WEBVTT

NOTE V2500 HPT Disk Stage 1 — Immersion Scan Tutorial (v2 educational)
NOTE Language: English (en)
NOTE Source: Rachel narration via ElevenLabs eleven_multilingual_v2
NOTE Total: 19 scenes · ~4:43 · 8 concept scenes + practical scan flow

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.500
V2500 HPT Disk · Stage 1.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.000
Immersion scan plan — the full walkthrough.

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.500
Before we start, why immersion testing?

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.000
Contact ultrasonic uses gel as the couplant.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The gel thickness changes from spot to spot,
and so does the energy entering the part.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Immersion solves this. The water layer becomes
a constant, repeatable, controllable couplant.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Every pulse sees the same acoustic path.

8
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.000
Water path: eight inches.
That number is not arbitrary.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.000
It matches the focal length of the transducer —
the IAE2P16679, five megahertz, eight-inch focal.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
With the disk surface at the focal point,
the beam diameter is minimum, resolution is maximum.

11
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.000
The probe fires straight down. But we want
a forty-five-degree shear wave inside the steel.

12
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.500
Snell's law gives us the trick.

13
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.500
We tilt the mirror so the beam enters the water
at eighteen point nine degrees.

14
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.000
At the water-to-steel interface, the angle bends,
and inside the steel the shear wave is exactly 45°.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Perpendicular to typical bore cracks.

16
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:18.000
Why scan each surface twice — plus 45° and minus 45°?

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Cracks have an orientation. A crack tilted
the wrong way is invisible to one beam direction.

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Scanning both directions turns the orientation problem
into guaranteed coverage. This is not optional.

19
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
It is the difference between inspection and theatre.

20
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.000
Anatomy of the disk. Five inspection zones —

21
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.000
E, the upper web transition.

22
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.500
A, the upper chamfer.

23
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.000
B, the upper land.

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.500
C, the bore entry.

25
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.000
D, the bore inner diameter.

26
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.000
Two-hundredths of an inch.
Scan increment — no larger.

27
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Index increment per revolution — no larger.

28
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Why? Because the focused beam diameter at the
focal point is about forty thousandths of an inch.

29
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Spacing each pass at half-beam guarantees no gap.

30
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Drop below this rule and you create blind alleys
for cracks to hide.

31
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.000
Full screen height. F-S-H. The amplifier maps
the strongest signal to one hundred percent.

32
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Calibration sets the reference reflector
to eighty percent FSH.

33
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Anything above twenty percent is flagged.
Anything below is noise.

34
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The twenty-percent floor sits above the typical
noise band, so we report real signals, not hiss.

35
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.000
Why calibrate at all? Because raw amplitude
has no meaning.

36
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
The number on the screen depends on probe wear,
water condition, even temperature.

37
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
So we anchor the system to a known reflector —
flat-bottom-hole number one.

38
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
And we adjust gain until that reflector
reads exactly eighty percent FSH.

39
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Every signal afterwards is read
relative to that anchor.

40
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.000
With the theory understood, we set up
the physical equipment.

41
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.500
Mount the disk on the turntable, fill the tank,
position the probe at the eight-inch water path.

42
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.000
Open the scan plan. Select the V2500
Stage 1 preset —

43
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.500
five zones populate automatically
from procedure NDIP 1226.

44
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.500
Zone E plus 45° pass.

45
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.000
The probe approaches at plus 45°,

46
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
the disk rotates 360°,

47
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
and the probe steps radially
in two-hundredths-inch increments.

48
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.000
Zone E minus 45° pass.

49
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
The mirror flips, and we repeat the sweep
in the opposite direction.

50
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Full bidirectional coverage.

51
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.500
Zones A through D.

52
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.000
Each zone is scanned twice — plus and minus 45°.

53
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.500
Ten passes in total.

54
00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.000
Two views of the same signal.

55
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
The A-scan is amplitude over time — what
the echo looks like at this one position.

56
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.500
The C-scan is a top-down map — peak amplitude
at every position the probe has visited.

57
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:13.500
The A-scan finds the indication.
The C-scan tells you where it is.

58
00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:18.000
Live C-scan capture. The colormap builds up
as the probe moves.

59
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Peak amplitude per position. Indications
above 20% FSH are flagged.

60
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Watch the data come in.

61
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.500
Inspection results. Five C-scan strips,
one per zone.

62
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.000
No reportable indications.
This disk is clean.

63
00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:42.000
Save the scan data. Enter the part
serial number and click Save.

64
00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.500
Inspection complete.

65
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.500
Five zones, ten passes, zero indications.
